+# ADR 0007: The upload lane — Apple's tooling moves the binary, the daemon writes small JSON, observation confirms
+
+Status: accepted (2026-09-02, plan tasks F0.4 / F1.1); implementation lands in
+plan tasks F1.2–F1.6 and is proven live in F1.9
+Date: 2026-09-02
+
+## Context
+
+Everything below is what exists at `integration/studio-wave1` `ff8f38d`,
+cited by file and line so the decision can be checked against the tree it
+was made on. Two code comments point at a `docs/plans` "Release Rail"
+document for numbered architecture decisions
+(`packages/contracts/src/v1/release-run.ts:19`,
+`packages/kernel/src/migrations/0020-release-runs.ts:4`); that document is
+not in the tree, so this ADR restates the decisions it relies on instead of
+citing it.
+
+- **The release machine.** Eight stages, `candidate → certified → archived →
+upload-approved → uploaded → processing → internal-testflight-available →
+device-smoke-passed` (`packages/contracts/src/v1/release.ts:18-27`,
+  `RELEASE_STAGE_ORDER_V1` at line 35), advanced exactly one stage at a time
+  ([ADR 0003](0003-release-state-reconciliation.md)). ADR 0003's stage
+  matrix makes `upload-approved` the human `testflight-upload` gate and
+  `uploaded`, `processing`, `internal-testflight-available` system-observed
+  facts.
+- **The run record already has an upload slot.**
+  `ReleaseRunUploadV1Schema` = `{ submittedAt, ascBuildId | null,
+confirmedAt | null }` (`release-run.ts:62-71`); `ReleaseRunV1.upload` is
+  nullable (line 89) and gated: the record is required at `uploaded`,
+  `ascBuildId` must be non-null at `uploaded`, `confirmedAt` must be non-null
+  at `internal-testflight-available` (lines 123-145). The comment at lines
+  64-68 states the intended confirmation mechanism: "the rail polls
+  `release.observe` until the build appears before advancing past upload".
+- **The archive record retains what the upload needs.**
+  `ReleaseRunArchiveV1Schema` carries `buildNumber`, `marketingVersion`,
+  `archiveDigest`, `exportedArtifactDigest`, `receiptDigest`
+  (`release-run.ts:53-60`).
+- **Build numbers are allocated, not inferred.**
+  `ReleaseBuildNumberAllocationV1` and `assertBuildNumberAllocationV1`
+  (`release-run.ts:218-266`) require every allocation for a bundle to be
+  new and strictly larger; migration 0020's `release_build_numbers` table is
+  append-only with `UNIQUE(bundle_id, build_number)` and reject-update /
+  reject-delete triggers (`0020-release-runs.ts:21-33` and the table DDL).
+  `release.archive` allocates at archive time via
+  `releaseBuildNumbers.allocateNext(bundleId, releaseRunId, observedAt)`
+  (`apps/daemon/src/release-archive-runtime.ts:898-902`) and bakes the
+  number in as `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` on the `xcodebuild archive`
+  command line (line 678). The ledger is empty on 2026-09-02, while Letters
+  already has a hand-shipped build 6 on TestFlight.
+- **The export options already name the upload path.**
+  `ReleaseExportOptionsConfigV1` has `destination: z.enum(["upload",
+"export"])` (`release-run.ts:286`), and its doc comment records the owner's
+  proven recipe for six apps: `xcodebuild archive … -allowProvisioningUpdates
+CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Automatic DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=<teamId>`, then `-exportArchive`
+  with `method: app-store-connect`, `destination: upload`, `signingStyle:
+automatic` (lines 272-280).
+- **`release.archive` (Wave 4) deliberately stops short of uploading.** It
+  runs `xcodegen generate → xcodebuild archive → xcodebuild -exportArchive`
+  under `process-supervisor`, outside the daemon's serial executor, and
+  advances `certified → archived` (`release-archive-runtime.ts:53-58`). Its
+  reviewed operator config hard-codes `destination: "export"` (line 128) and
+  refuses anything else: `destination must be "export" -- uploading to App
+Store Connect is Wave 5, gated on the "upload-approved" owner approval, and
+this daemon build never performs it` (lines 224-228); the handler refuses a
+  non-export request with `release.archive-destination-not-supported` (lines
+  843-848). The plist renderer's comment is explicit about what comes next:
+  "The owner's proven ExportOptions.plist shape (memory, six apps):
+  `method: app-store-connect`, `signingStyle: automatic`, plus
+  `uploadSymbols: true` -- **Wave 5 will set `destination: upload`**; this
+  wave always writes `destination: export`" (lines 353-355,
+  `renderExportOptionsPlistV1` at 356). The `.xcarchive` and the exported
+  artifact are retained and digested (lines 714-770).
+- **The wire protocol already has the operations.** `release.upload` and
+  `release.submit` exist with payload `{ releaseRunId, expectedRevision,
+approvalId }` (`packages/contracts/src/v1/command-protocol.ts:693-711`); the
+  module comment says "`release.upload`/`release.submit` each require an
+  `approvalId`: the owner gate is `upload-approved` … everything up to and
+  including `archived` may run unattended, but nothing that crosses to Apple
+  does so without an explicit human approval" (lines 639-650). The daemon
+  answers both with `command.operation-not-yet-implemented`
+  (`apps/daemon/src/command-runtime.ts:2027-2038`). No `release.approve` or
+  `release.confirm` operation exists.
+- **Approvals can already be release-scoped.** Migration 0020 rebuilt
+  `approvals` so a subject may be `{projectId, releaseId}` with
+  `taskId`/`attemptId` null (`0020-release-runs.ts:33` ff. and the
+  `approvals_subject_matches_attempt` trigger); rows are `single-use` or
+  `standing`, move only `active → consumed | revoked | expired`, and a
+  consumed row names its `consumed_by_effect_id`. Exactly one action policy
+  uses the release scope: `apple.upload-build` — provider `apple`, resource
+  type `apple.build`, required bindings `planDigest`, `commit`,
+  `buildIdentityDigest` (`packages/kernel/src/effect-repositories.ts:160-165`).
+- **The App Store Connect adapter is GET-only by construction.** "Every
+  method issues bounded GETs only. There is no code path that builds a
+  POST/PATCH/DELETE, no request body, and no write endpoint string anywhere
+  in this package" (`packages/asc-adapter/src/observer.ts:35-43`;
+  `http.ts:20`). Responses are capped at 1 MiB (`http.ts:37`). Tokens are
+  ES256 JWTs of at most 20 minutes, minted only for
+  `api.appstoreconnect.apple.com`, from a `.p8` that is zeroized after
+  signing (`auth.ts:22-26`, `createAscJwtAuthorization`); an optional
+  per-request `scope` claim exists but is off by default because it is
+  unproven live (`auth.ts:52-58`). The shared provider HTTP method type is
+  `"GET" | "POST" | "PUT"` — no `PATCH`
+  (`packages/provider-http-adapters/src/http.ts:62`). The transport reads
+  every body under a hard byte cap (`packages/provider-transport/src/index.ts:126-146`).
+  `release.observe` has taken a live observation through the daemon: 25
+  GETs, 25 × 200, 12 apps, 9.2 s, persisted and served digest-bound
+  ([`release-rail.md`](../operations/release-rail.md), 2026-08-18); Apple
+  reported `user-hour-lim:3600` on that key
+  ([`asc-live-read.md`](../operations/asc-live-read.md)).
+- **The daemon has no network listener.** Its only server is a Unix domain
+  socket (`apps/daemon/src/unix-command-server.ts:3,721,782`, `node:net`,
+  `server.listen(socketPath)`); there is no HTTP server anywhere in
+  `apps/daemon/src`, so nothing Apple could call back into exists.
+- **A consumer of the availability event already exists.**
+  `packages/website-lifecycle/src/index.ts` consumes
+  `release.testflight-available` (lines 142, 183, 204) and plans an
+  approval-required website PR from it.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **`release.upload` is a second `xcodebuild -exportArchive` of the retained
+   `.xcarchive` with `destination: upload`.** The ExportOptions plist is the
+   archive step's plist (`method: app-store-connect`, `teamID`,
+   `signingStyle: automatic`, `uploadSymbols: true`) with `destination`
+   changed to `upload` and `manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber: false` added.
+   Apple's tooling moves the binary, exactly as it did for the owner's six
+   hand-shipped apps; **the daemon's bounded HTTP transport never carries a
+   binary.** The step runs under `process-supervisor` outside the serial
+   executor, with the same argv-array-never-a-shell discipline, scratch
+   directory, timeouts, and receipt as `release.archive`. Preconditions,
+   checked inside the serial executor before the subprocess starts: the run
+   is at `upload-approved` at the expected revision; the retained
+   `.xcarchive` and exported artifact still hash to `archive.archiveDigest`
+   and `archive.exportedArtifactDigest`; the approval named in the payload is
+   active, release-scoped to this run's `{projectId, releaseId}`, bound to
+   `apple.upload-build`, and bound to this run's source commit and archive
+   identity. Success writes the `upload` record with `submittedAt` and a
+   null `ascBuildId`; the stage does **not** advance on the subprocess's
+   exit code — only observation advances it (item 5). Failure writes a
+   durable revision with the captured stderr tail in `notes`, as
+   `release.archive` does.
+2. **Build numbers are owned by the daemon, and Xcode is told so.**
+   `ReleaseExportOptionsConfigV1` gains `manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber:
+false` (a literal, so no config can turn it on), the plist renders it, and
+   the JSON schema is regenerated (F1.5). The allocator's floor becomes
+   `max(highest ledger allocation for the bundle, latest build number
+Apple has reported for the bundle in a persisted `release.observe`
+observation) + 1`; the ledger stays append-only and strictly increasing per
+   bundle, so a number is never reused. The floor exists because Letters is
+   at build 6 with an empty ledger: without it the first factory allocation
+   would be `1` and Apple would reject the upload. Because the number is
+   baked at archive time, **a re-upload after any failure or stall is a new
+   archive with a new number**, never a re-send of the same binary.
+3. **App Store Connect writes are small JSON only, through the adapter.**
+   `ProviderHttpMethod` gains `PATCH`; `packages/asc-adapter` gains a write
+   path with typed Apple error envelopes and a bounded 429 backoff, over the
+   same just-in-time JWT and byte-capped transport as reads (F1.2). Exactly
+   three writes are in scope for the upload lane: `PATCH /v1/builds/{id}`
+   (`usesNonExemptEncryption`), `POST /v1/betaBuildLocalizations` (what's
+   new), and `POST /v1/betaGroups/{id}/relationships/builds` (internal
+   groups only). Every write is a kernel external effect with a
+   release-scoped subject; it is refused unless it consumes an active,
+   single-use approval. No standing approval covers Apple.
+4. **Approvals are minted by a new `release.approve` operation.**
+   `release.approve` (CLI `factory release approve <run> --gate
+upload-approved`) is issued by a human actor only and mints the approval
+   set the lane will consume for one run: one single-use, expiring approval
+   for the binary upload and one per follow-on JSON write, each bound to the
+   `apple.upload-build` action policy's subject scope (`{projectId,
+releaseId}`), to the run's source commit, and to its archive identity;
+   registered through the kernel's existing `registerApproval` path with
+   the release-scoped branch migration 0020 enabled. `release.upload`'s
+   existing `approvalId` payload field names the upload approval. A machine
+   cannot mint, extend, or reuse one; an expired or consumed approval is
+   refused before any subprocess or request starts. `release.approve` also
+   advances `archived → upload-approved`, the human `testflight-upload` gate
+   of ADR 0003's matrix, so the stage and the approval are one recorded act.
+5. **Confirmation is by observation; there is no webhook receiver.** A new
+   `release.confirm` operation polls a bounded per-app builds read (the
+   adapter's `listBuilds` for the one app — not the portfolio-wide
+   `release.observe`, whose 25 GETs per observation would exhaust the
+   3,600-per-hour limit at any useful cadence) until a build whose
+   `buildNumber` equals the run's allocated number appears for the bundle;
+   it then records `upload.ascBuildId` and advances `uploaded`, records
+   Apple's `processingState`/`internalBuildState` and advances `processing`,
+   performs the three JSON writes of item 3 once processing is valid, and
+   on `internalBuildState` reporting the build in internal testing sets
+   `upload.confirmedAt`, advances `internal-testflight-available`, and emits
+   `release.testflight-available` (a `LifecycleEventV1` carrying the
+   `releaseId`, which `packages/website-lifecycle` consumes). Every advance
+   is one stage through `assertReleaseRunAdvancement` under the run's CAS
+   revision, and each observation the daemon acts on is itself persisted
+   (migration 0014's append-only `asc_release_observations` or a per-app
+   equivalent). A build that has not appeared after 24 hours, or that Apple
+   reports as failed or invalid, ends the run's upload attempt with a
+   durable note; recovery is a new archive with a new build number (item 2).
+6. **`release.submit` is reserved for App Store review submission** (plan
+   task F4.5), behind its own owner-only gate and its own approval; it is
+   not part of the upload lane and it does not touch TestFlight. ADR 0003's
+   target is `ios-internal-testflight` and its terminal stage is
+   `device-smoke-passed`; a store-review submission needs a stage or target
+   extension that ADR 0003 and ADR 0005 explicitly left unmodelled, which is
+   a later ADR, not this one.
+7. **The App Store Connect Build Uploads API is explicitly not built.**
+   Apple's API-driven upload — the client itself reserving an upload,
+   streaming the binary in parts to Apple, and committing it — would have to
+   run through the daemon's transport, which is designed and bounded for
+   small JSON (item 3's constraints, the 1 MiB response cap, byte-capped
+   body reads, a 20-minute token). Nothing in this repository moves a binary
+   over HTTP, and this decision keeps it that way.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **Contracts:** `ReleaseExportOptionsConfigV1` gains the
+  `manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber: false` literal; two wire operations are
+  added (`release.approve`, `release.confirm`) and `release.upload` gains a
+  handler; `COMMAND_OPERATIONS_V1` and the checked-in JSON schemas are
+  regenerated. `ProviderHttpMethod` gains `PATCH` (a shared type; the Jira
+  and GitHub adapters are unaffected but their tests must keep proving they
+  never emit it).
+- **Daemon config:** the reviewed `APP_FACTORY_RELEASE_CONFIG` allowlist
+  stays fail-closed. `release.archive` keeps writing `destination: export`
+  and keeps refusing anything else; the upload step gets its own reviewed
+  section of that file naming the one permitted destination, so a wire
+  request can never choose between exporting and uploading.
+- **No new migration in F1.** The `approvals` table already accepts
+  release-scoped rows, `release_runs.stage` already checks all eight stages,
+  and `release_build_numbers` already exists. The next migration remains
+  `0021` (ADR 0006).
+- **Evidence:** the upload subprocess receipt, the run's revisions, the
+  consumed approvals (`consumed_by_effect_id`), and the persisted
+  observations together are the record of an upload; the first live run is
+  written up in `docs/operations/testflight-upload.md` by F1.9 the way
+  `release-rail.md` wrote up the first live read.
+- **Studio:** the release rail gains the `upload-approved` gate as a gold ◆
+  (the human's) and the `uploaded → processing →
+internal-testflight-available` progression in cyan (the machine's), with the
+  observation's `observedAt` shown, never an estimate (F1.10).
+- **The daemon stays Unix-socket-only.** No listener is added for Apple.
+- **Rate limits are a design input:** per-app polling only (item 5);
+  cadence is an open item below.
+- **Role and key:** the key in Keychain is an App Manager team key
+  (`asc-live-read.md`) rotated by F0.7a. Whether that role is sufficient for
+  the three writes is established by the one live `PATCH` F1.2 runs behind
+  an approval, not assumed.
+
+## Alternatives rejected
+
+- **Build Uploads API through the daemon's transport.** Rejected (decision
+  item 7). It would add a credential-holding, resumable, multipart binary
+  path to a transport built for small JSON, to replace a step Apple's tooling
+  already performs and the owner has already exercised six times. Revisit
+  only if `xcodebuild -exportArchive` with `destination: upload` is shown to
+  fail under the daemon's non-interactive supervision (for example a
+  Keychain prompt), with that failure recorded as evidence.
+- **`altool` or Transporter.** Rejected: `altool` is deprecated by Apple;
+  Transporter is a separate interactive application; `-exportArchive
+upload` uses the same Apple upload machinery with the signing identity that
+  the archive step already proved usable.
+- **A webhook receiver for Apple's build-processing events.** Rejected for
+  this wave: the daemon has no network listener and adding one is a new
+  inbound surface with its own authentication; polling a per-app read is
+  sufficient for one build at a time. F1.8 is a design note only.
+- **Letting Xcode manage the build number** (`manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber:
+true`). Rejected: the number would be chosen at upload time by Xcode from
+  Apple's state, the ledger would record something else, and "the build
+  number is allocated by the rail, recorded durably, and never inferred"
+  (migration 0020's own doc comment) would be false.
+- **A ledger-only floor.** Rejected: Letters' build 6 exists outside the
+  ledger; ignoring observed builds guarantees a collision on the first
+  upload.
+- **One standing approval per release covering every Apple write.**
+  Rejected for this wave: a retry loop could then write repeatedly without
+  a human seeing the count; single-use approvals make every write a counted,
+  human-issued act. A standing scope may be reconsidered once the lane has
+  run live more than once.
+- **Advancing to `uploaded` on the subprocess's success.** Rejected: an exit
+  code says the tool finished, not that Apple has the build;
+  `ReleaseRunUploadV1.ascBuildId` is required at `uploaded` precisely so
+  that the stage cannot be reached without Apple's own identifier.
+- **Folding App Store review submission into `release.upload`.** Rejected:
+  different owner gate, different Apple resources (`appStoreVersions`), and
+  a stage extension (decision item 6).
+
+## Not decided / open
+
+- **ASC webhooks** — design note only (F1.8); no listener is planned.
+- **External TestFlight** groups, beta App Review submission, and public
+  links — out of scope; internal groups only.
+- **Polling cadence and budget for `release.confirm`** — bounded per-app
+  reads are decided; the interval, the total request budget per run, and
+  how the budget shares the 3,600-per-hour key limit with `release.observe`
+  and the F1.11 credential probe are F1.6's call and must be recorded in the
+  run's notes.
+- **Action-policy partition for the follow-on writes** — whether the three
+  JSON writes ride on `apple.upload-build` with `apple.build` resource keys
+  or receive their own action names is F1.2/F1.3's call; either way each
+  write is a release-scoped, single-use, human-minted approval.
+- **The JWT `scope` claim for writes** — attractive for `PATCH`/`POST`,
+  off by default because unproven live (`auth.ts:52-58`); to be proven or
+  left off, never assumed.
+- **Key role sufficiency** — App Manager is proven for reads only.
+- **What `exportedArtifactDigest` re-verification means** — ADR 0003 left
+  the exported artifact ungated to any stage; whether the upload
+  precondition checks only the `.xcarchive` or both is settled in F1.4 with
+  the evidence of what `-exportArchive upload` actually consumes.
+- **Representation of an Apple-side rejection** (`processingState` failed or
+  invalid, or a 24-hour stall): the run keeps its stage and carries a durable
+  note; whether a distinct terminal marker on the run is warranted is
+  decided in F1.6.
+- **Overflow build host** — deferred by owner decision F0.10.
